# Testing

## Framework semantics

CakePHP testing is PHPUnit-oriented with Table tests (`TestCase` + fixtures), integration/controller tests (`IntegrationTestTrait`), and optional factories when the project already uses them. Prefer CakePHP fixture strategy over inventing Pest/Laravel testing stacks unless the project already uses them.

## Required behavior

- Prefer deterministic fixtures/data; isolate tests from production network services.
- Choose test type by boundary: pure unit vs Table persistence vs HTTP integration.
- Table tests assert validation, application rules, finders, and save/delete behavior through Table APIs.
- Integration tests hit routes/controllers with session/CSRF helpers consistent with the app.
- Refresh schema/fixtures the CakePHP way the project already uses (`fixtures`, migrations in test bootstrap) — do not invent ad-hoc DB resets.
- Do not impose Pest unless the project already uses it.

## Anti-patterns

- Tests that require production network services without isolation
- Asserting only controller redirects while skipping Table rule failures
- Inventing Laravel feature-test helpers as the CakePHP default

## Review checklist

- [ ] Persistence invariants covered at Table layer when changed
- [ ] HTTP flows covered with IntegrationTestTrait when routes/actions change
- [ ] Fixtures/factories match project conventions and stay deterministic
